    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I've got a heifer that just came out of rumenotomy about 6 hours ago. I need a clear protocol for what I should be looking out for over the next few days regarding the incision site and any internal signs of trouble or infection. Also, what's the plan for getting her back on feed?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A heifer post-rumenotomy, that's a delicate situation. You'll want to keep a close eye on the incision site, making sure it's healing properly, not too much bleeding, and no signs of swelling or redness. It's a good idea to monitor the site for any discharge, and if you notice any, you can treat it with a topical antibiotic ointment. You can also check the incision site for any signs of separation or dehiscence, which would require immediate veterinary attention.\n\nAs for internal signs of trouble or infection, you'll want to monitor the heifer's appetite, water intake, and overall behavior. If she's showing signs of lethargy, loss of appetite, or decreased water intake, it could be a sign of internal complications. You should also check her rectal temperature regularly, as a fever can indicate infection.\n\nNow, regarding getting her back on feed, it's essential to do it gradually and safely. You can start by offering her a small amount of hay or grain, and gradually increase the amount over the next 24-48 hours. It's also crucial to ensure she's getting enough water, so make sure her water bucket is full and easily accessible. You",
      "source": "target",
